        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What PSI should I use to pressure wash a patio?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Use the lowest pressure that cleans effectively. Cured poured concrete tolerates typical homeowner gas washer pressure, while pavers and especially soft natural stone need less. Test on a hidden spot and increase gradually, and use a cleaner so you do not need to rely on pressure alone."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you pressure wash pavers without removing the sand?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Some sand loss is almost unavoidable, but you can minimise it. Keep pressure moderate, use a surface cleaner that keeps moving, and angle a wand across the surface rather than into joints. Plan to refill the joints with jointing or polymeric sand once the patio is dry."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a surface cleaner good for a patio?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. It gives an even finish and contains spray, which matters close to the house. Choose a deck size your washer can spin properly, often 12 to 16 inches for patios, lower the pressure for pavers and stone, and use the wand for edges and corners."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
