    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A patio is not a driveway. It is usually smaller, broken up by furniture and planters, often laid in pavers or stone rather than a single slab, and close to the house where overspray lands on windows and doors. That changes the approach. The right pressure for a broom-finished concrete patio can etch soft limestone and blow the sand out of every paver joint. Identify what your patio is made of, choose the pressure and tool to match, and protect the joints and the surroundings. Then a surface cleaner and a careful rinse do most of the work.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="material" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Know What Your Patio Is Made Of
          </h2>

          <p>
            Poured concrete is the most forgiving patio surface. A cured broom-finished or smooth slab handles typical homeowner gas washer pressure and a surface cleaner well, provided you keep the tool moving. Stamped and coloured concrete needs more care, because the colour is often a surface layer or a sealer that aggressive cleaning can strip.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concrete pavers are dense and durable, but they sit on a sand bed with sand-filled joints. The pavers themselves tolerate moderate pressure; the joints do not. Pavers are also often sealed, and an older sealer can turn patchy or white when cleaned aggressively.
          </p>

          <p>
            Natural stone varies the most. Granite and dense slate tolerate moderate pressure. Limestone, sandstone and travertine are softer and more porous, and high pressure can erode their surface, open pores and leave visible tracks. Brick sits in between, and its mortar joints can be damaged as easily as the face.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are not sure what you have, test on a hidden corner at low pressure and increase gradually. The right pressure is the lowest that cleans effectively. More is not better on any patio material.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
