            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Prep the Patio and the Surroundings
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clear everything off the patio: furniture, planters, grills, mats and toys. Furniture can be cleaned separately and should not be blasted on the patio surface. Sweep or blow off leaves, soil and grit so they do not get trapped under a surface cleaner or ground into the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protect the house. Close windows and doors, cover outdoor outlets and light fittings with plastic and tape, and keep the spray away from door seals and weep holes. A surface cleaner contains most spray, but the wand used for edges and rinsing throws water sideways and up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wet down nearby plants and lawn before you start if you plan to use any cleaner, and cover delicate plants. Pre-wetting dilutes runoff and reduces the chance of chemical damage. Rinse them again afterwards.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan where the water will go. Work from the high side of the patio toward the low side or drain, so dirty water runs over uncleaned areas rather than over finished ones. On a patio that slopes toward the house, as some do, take extra care with the final rinse to push water away from the foundation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the weather. You want a dry day with a dry day following if you plan to re-sand or seal. Cleaning in direct, hot sun can make cleaners dry before they work, and cold weather slows drying for days.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pretreat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Pre-Treat Stains and Choose a Cleaner
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pressure alone is the wrong tool for most patio grime. Algae, moss, lichen and mildew come back quickly if you only blast the surface layer, and higher pressure to chase them down is what damages softer materials. A suitable cleaner applied first does most of the work and lets you use lower pressure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Match the cleaner to the material and the stain. A general concrete and masonry cleaner suits most organic grime on concrete and pavers. Oil and grease need a degreaser. Rust needs a rust remover formulated for masonry. On natural stone, especially limestone and travertine, avoid acidic cleaners, which can etch the surface; use products labelled safe for natural stone. Our <a href="/best-paver-cleaner/">paver cleaner rankings</a> cover options by surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Apply the cleaner with a pump sprayer or through your washer's soap system, following the label for dilution and dwell time. Keep the surface wet during the dwell time so the cleaner does not dry in place. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-use-detergent-in-a-pressure-washer/">using detergent in a pressure washer</a> covers the soap nozzle and siphon setup.
          </p>

          <p>
            For stubborn spots, a stiff nylon brush during the dwell time helps more than extra pressure. Avoid wire brushes on pavers and stone, which can scratch the surface and leave metal particles that rust.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="clean" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Clean the Main Area
          </h2>

          <p>
            A surface cleaner is the best tool for the open field of a patio. It holds the nozzles at a fixed height, contains the spray and leaves an even finish, which matters on a patio people will see up close. Choose a deck size your washer can spin properly; patios often suit a 12 to 16 inch deck that is easier to steer around posts and steps. Our guide to <a href="/what-size-surface-cleaner-do-i-need/">surface cleaner sizing</a> explains how to match it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Move slowly and overlap each pass by about a third to a half of the deck width, working in straight lanes. Keep the deck flat. On pavers and stone, lower the pressure if your washer allows it, or use a deck with nozzles sized for lower output. Our <a href="/how-to-use-a-pressure-washer-surface-cleaner/">surface cleaner technique guide</a> covers pace, overlap and edging in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do not have a surface cleaner, use a 25-degree or 40-degree tip, held at a consistent distance and moved in even, overlapping sweeps. Keep the wand angled slightly so the spray pushes dirt across the surface rather than straight down into the joints. Avoid 0-degree tips and turbo nozzles on pavers and soft stone; our comparison of a <a href="/surface-cleaner-vs-turbo-nozzle/">surface cleaner vs turbo nozzle</a> explains why.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean edges, steps and corners with the wand after the field, feathering the spray into the deck-cleaned area so there is no visible boundary. Work carefully around the base of walls and along the patio's edge restraint, where joints and bedding sand are most exposed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our <a href="/best-surface-cleaner-for-pressure-washer/">surface cleaner rankings</a> list which attachments suit electric and lower-pressure washers, which are often the better match for paver and stone patios.
          </p>

          <p>
            Take breaks on bigger patios. Fatigue makes people speed up and tilt the deck, which is exactly what causes uneven patches. Stopping every twenty minutes or so to move the hose and check the finished area keeps the pace consistent from the first lane to the last.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="joints" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Protect the Paver Joints
          </h2>

          <p>
            Joint sand is what holds a paver patio together. It locks the pavers against each other, keeps weeds out and stops pavers from shifting. Pressure washing almost always removes some of it, and aggressive cleaning can strip joints to the bedding sand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep pressure moderate and avoid aiming the spray straight down into joints. With a surface cleaner, keep moving so the jets never dwell over a joint line. With a wand, angle the spray across the surface rather than into it. Accept that some sand loss is normal and plan to replace it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the joints contain polymeric sand that is cracked, crumbling or full of weeds, cleaning is a good time to remove it and start fresh. Polymeric sand that is still intact and hard can usually be cleaned over gently and topped up where needed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch the bedding sand as well. If water is blasting out from under pavers or you can see the base, you are using too much pressure for the joint condition. Stop, reduce pressure and finish more gently.
          </p>

          <p>
            Edge restraints deserve a look while the joints are exposed. The plastic, metal or concrete edging along the patio's border is what keeps the outer pavers from creeping outward. If it has lifted, cracked or pulled away, fix it before re-sanding, or the new joint sand will open up again as the pavers spread.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rinse" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Rinse and Let It Dry
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rinse the whole patio from the high side to the low side with a wide nozzle once cleaning is done. A surface cleaner leaves loosened dirt in a slurry; without a rinse, it dries as a haze. Rinse cleaner residue thoroughly too, since many cleaners can leave a film that affects re-sanding or sealing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rinse plants, lawn edges and the base of the house wall again, and wipe or rinse any spatter from windows, doors and siding.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let the patio dry fully before judging the result or doing anything else. Wet pavers and stone look darker and more even than they are. Uneven areas or streaks show as the surface dries, and a light second pass on just those areas usually fixes them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drying time depends on weather and material. Porous stone and pavers can hold moisture well below the surface after looking dry. If you plan to re-sand with polymeric sand or apply a sealer, follow the product's dry time, which is commonly at least 24 hours and often longer in cool or humid weather.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check for standing water as the patio dries. Puddles that stay long after the rest of the surface is dry point to low spots or settled pavers. Cleaning is a good moment to note them, since a paver that has sunk can be lifted, the bedding sand topped up and the paver relaid before you re-sand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Re-Sand, Seal and Keep It Clean
          </h2>

          <p>
            Refill paver joints once the patio and joints are completely dry. Regular jointing sand is the simplest option; polymeric sand hardens into a firmer joint that resists washout and weeds better. Follow the product's instructions closely, especially for sweeping off residue before wetting, or it can leave a haze. Our <a href="/best-polymeric-sand/">polymeric sand rankings</a> compare products.
          </p>

          <p>
            Consider sealing after cleaning and re-sanding. A sealer can make future cleaning easier, slow organic growth and, with some products, help stabilise joint sand. Pavers and stone need sealers made for them, and the surface must be fully dry. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-seal-pavers/">sealing pavers</a> covers application.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concrete patios can be sealed too. A penetrating sealer protects without changing the look much, while a film-forming sealer adds sheen or a wet look. Either way, an even clean is the starting point, because sealer locks in whatever is on the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep it clean between washes. Sweep regularly, rinse off spills promptly and clear leaves that sit and stain. A patio that gets light maintenance needs a full pressure wash far less often, which is better for the joints and the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan on a full clean once a year or so for most patios, or more often in shaded, damp spots where algae grows quickly. Annual cleaning at modest pressure does far less harm than a single aggressive clean every few years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using driveway pressure on stone or pavers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The pressure that suits a cured concrete driveway can erode soft stone and blow out paver joints. Identify the material first, test on a hidden spot and use the lowest pressure that cleans. Let a cleaner do the heavy lifting.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Aiming the spray straight into joints</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Spraying down into paver joints strips joint sand and can undermine the bedding sand. Keep a surface cleaner moving and angle a wand across the surface, then plan to top up or replace the joint sand afterwards.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using acidic cleaners on natural stone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Limestone, travertine and marble-like stones react with acid, which can etch and dull the surface. Choose products labelled safe for natural stone, follow the label and test on a hidden area before cleaning the whole patio.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Re-sanding or sealing before the patio is dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Polymeric sand and sealers need dry joints and a dry surface. Applying them to damp pavers can cause haze, poor curing or a cloudy finish. Wait for the product's dry time, commonly at least 24 hours, and longer in cool weather.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping protection around the house</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Overspray from the wand can force water past door seals, into outlets and behind siding. Close windows and doors, cover outlets and light fittings, and keep the spray directed away from the house wall.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What PSI should I use to pressure wash a patio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use the lowest pressure that cleans effectively. Cured poured concrete tolerates typical homeowner gas washer pressure, while pavers and especially soft natural stone need less. Test on a hidden spot and increase gradually, and use a cleaner so you do not need to rely on pressure alone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you pressure wash pavers without removing the sand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some sand loss is almost unavoidable, but you can minimise it. Keep pressure moderate, use a surface cleaner that keeps moving, and angle a wand across the surface rather than into joints. Plan to refill the joints with jointing or polymeric sand once the patio is dry.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a surface cleaner good for a patio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. It gives an even finish and contains spray, which matters close to the house. Choose a deck size your washer can spin properly, often 12 to 16 inches for patios, lower the pressure for pavers and stone, and use the wand for edges and corners.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long after pressure washing pavers can I seal them?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Follow the sealer manufacturer's instructions, which commonly call for at least 24 hours of drying and often longer in cool or humid weather. Pavers can hold moisture below the surface after looking dry, and sealing damp pavers can cause clouding or poor adhesion.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I pressure wash a natural stone patio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, carefully. Dense stones like granite tolerate moderate pressure; softer stones like limestone, sandstone and travertine need low pressure and a stone-safe cleaner. Avoid acidic products on calcareous stone and test on a hidden area first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What cleaner should I use before pressure washing a patio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the stain and material. A concrete and masonry cleaner handles most organic grime, a degreaser handles oil, and a masonry-safe rust remover handles rust. On natural stone, use products labelled stone-safe. Follow the label's dilution and dwell time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should a patio be pressure washed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most patios, once a year is enough, more often in damp, shaded areas where algae grows quickly. Regular sweeping and prompt rinsing of spills reduce how often you need a full clean, which is better for joints and softer surfaces.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

